**assistant**:
> To achieve this, you'll want to read your CSV file into a Pandas DataFrame, then convert it into a Pandas Series with the date as the index and the 'close' price as the data. Here's how you can do it:
> 
> 1. First, read the CSV file using `pandas.read_csv`, ensuring the 'timestamp' column is parsed as datetime objects.
> 
> 2. Set the 'timestamp' column as the index of the DataFrame.
> 
> 3. Extract the 'close' column as a Pandas Series.
> 
> Here's how you can implement these steps in code:
> 
> ```python
> import pandas as pd
> 
> # Read the CSV file
> df = pd.read_csv('path/to/your/file.csv', parse_dates=['timestamp'])
> 
> # Set the 'timestamp' column as the index
> df.set_index('timestamp', inplace=True)
> 
> # Extract the 'close' column as a Pandas Series
> px = df['close']
> 
> # Rename the index to 'Date' for consistency
> px.index.name = 'Date'
> 
> # Print the resulting Series
> print(px)
> ```
> 
> Ensure that `'path/to/your/file.csv'` is replaced with the actual path to your CSV file. After running this code, `px` will be a Pandas Series that looks similar to what you have for parquet files, with the date as the index and the 'close' price as the Series values.
