> The Date column there has a full DateTime. What if I just want it to show the Date?
**assistant**:
> If you want to keep only the date portion (without the time part) of the 'timestamp', you can use the `.dt.date` accessor to convert it to just the date. Here’s how you can modify the previous code to achieve that:
> 
> ```python
> import pandas as pd
> 
> # Read the CSV file
> df = pd.read_csv('path/to/your/file.csv', parse_dates=['timestamp'])
> 
> # Set the 'timestamp' column as the index
> df.set_index('timestamp', inplace=True)
> 
> # Extract the 'close' column as a Pandas Series
> px = df['close']
> 
> # Rename the index to 'Date' for consistency and convert to just the date
> px.index = px.index.date
> px.index.name = 'Date'
> 
> # Print the resulting Series
> print(px)
> ```
> 
> In this updated code:
> - `px.index = px.index.date` converts the DateTime index to a date-only format.
> This way, your output will show only the date in the format you're looking for, while the values remain the closing prices.
